INT. CANDLELIT SALON - 1790S PARIS - NIGHT

A narrow room of mirrors. One candelabrum. Shutters closed against the street.

JOSEPHINE LAURENT, thirties, dressed in mourning black, stands at a writing desk. MR. CALLOWAY, fifties, an English banker in a travel coat, waits by the door, hat in hand.

He clears his throat. She doesn't turn.

CALLOWAY
Madame.

She finishes sealing a letter. Presses the wax. Sets the signet down.

JOSEPHINE
You are late.

CALLOWAY
The barricades, Madame.

JOSEPHINE
You are English. You could have walked through them.

He permits himself a small smile. She turns at last, studies him.

CALLOWAY
You have it?

She slides open a drawer. Takes out a leather folio. Holds it against her chest.

JOSEPHINE
First, my passage.

He pulls a folded paper from his coat. She takes it, reads. Her shoulders loosen a fraction. Then she tightens again.

JOSEPHINE (CONT'D)
This is for one.

CALLOWAY
It is.

JOSEPHINE
I have a daughter.

He says nothing. His jaw works. She lifts the folio, as if to put it back in the drawer.

CALLOWAY
Madame.

JOSEPHINE
You said two.

CALLOWAY
I said I would try.

She closes the drawer on the folio. Turns her back. He crosses the room in three strides, grips her elbow, then thinks better of it and lets go.

CALLOWAY (CONT'D)
Forgive me.

JOSEPHINE
(without turning)
Get out.

He doesn't move. Shouts rise in the street. A bell. Candles gutter. She flinches. He steps toward her and, this time, stops short, hat crushed in his hands.

CALLOWAY
I will find a second passage.

JOSEPHINE
When?

CALLOWAY
Three days.

JOSEPHINE
We do not have three days.

He takes out his pocketbook. Counts notes onto the desk. A lot of them. She watches them stack.

CALLOWAY
For the second ticket. Buy it from whoever you must.

She looks at the money. At him. Slowly, she opens the drawer again and hands him the folio.

He tucks it inside his coat. She pockets the notes. For a moment neither moves.

CALLOWAY (CONT'D)
Thank you.

JOSEPHINE
Do not thank me. Be quick.

He bows, stiff. Goes to the door. Hesitates with a hand on the latch.

CALLOWAY
She will be safe.

JOSEPHINE
She will be if you are honest.

He nods once and slips out. She blows out the candles, one by one, until only the glow of the street shows through the shutters.
